1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Where did the requests go? Stored rows, a dashboard rule, and two missing tickets.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,000
The lab's stored rows: four tickets and two agents.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:12,500
The dashboard shows only 1001. The promise needs 1001, 1004, and 1009.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,766
Yet 1004 and 1009 are right there in the table. Nothing was deleted.

5
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:25,666
1001, 1004, and 1009 survive. The status rule matches the promise.

6
00:00:26,166 --> 00:00:28,866
Pair every active ticket with every agent: 3 × 2 = 6 pairs.

7
00:00:29,366 --> 00:00:31,366
Most pairs are nonsense: 1001 sits next to Agent 202. The ON condition decides which pairs survive.

8
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:34,266
Test each pair: does assignee_id equal agent_id?

9
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:38,266
NULL = 201 is not true; it is unknown. Only pairs that test true are kept.

10
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:40,766
One pair survives: 1001 with Agent 201. That is the whole dashboard.

11
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:45,666
This is what the rule means. PostgreSQL need not build all pairs to compute it.

12
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:48,166
Change the data, not the rule: ticket 1004 now has assignee 202.

13
00:00:48,666 --> 00:00:51,166
Now 1004 finds a match and appears on the dashboard.

14
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:54,666
1009 is still unassigned, so the promise is still broken.

15
00:00:55,166 --> 00:00:59,866
Undo the change. Assigning tickets just to make them visible hides the real problem.

16
00:01:00,366 --> 00:01:02,866
An active ticket stays even when no agent matches. The gap is labeled, not invented.

17
00:01:03,366 --> 00:01:06,366
No fake agent, no edited rows, no restored backup. Next week this becomes a LEFT JOIN.

18
00:01:07,966 --> 00:01:17,966
Remember: A stored row and a query result can contain different rows. A product pairs every ticket with every agent; a join keeps matching pairs. NULL matches nothing, so an inner match drops unassigned tickets. Fix the rule, not the data: keep the ticket, label the missing agent. Check IDs, not just counts, against the promise.

